the federal government is extending the  nationwide eviction moratorium through  june 30th  it was supposed to expire on wednesday  the i-team's david charns joining us in  studio this afternoon with what this  means  for nevada well christian it's certainly  good news for people who can't pay the  rent because of the pandemic but  remember that all that back rent will  eventually  have to be paid the moratorium applies  to most tenants and allows people to  remain in their homes  even if they cannot pay rent right now  now remember this isn't automatic you  have to make less than a hundred  thousand dollars a year  show covet has impacted your income  prove you have nowhere else to go  and also sign a declaration and then  give that to your landlord  the executive director of the nevada  state department association says the  best thing you can do right now as a  tenant  is talk to your landlord about making  payments when you can  i think communication with your landlord  is imperative it is  crucial we want to know you know whether  or not you're going to be making a rent  payment because right now if we haven't  heard from you we're assuming that  you're not  of course rent coming up on thursday  april 1st  now nevada's state level election  moratorium which basically provides the  same protections  as the federal one that one expires  wednesday  spokesperson for governor sisilak tells  me that their office is now determining  whether or not to extend the state's  moratorium but essentially the federal  cdc moratorium  does the same thing now there is money  available from right here in clark  county to help you pay your rent  it's called the chap program that is  c-h-a-p  you can find a link to all that and  other resources right now on  eightnewsnow.com  i'm david charns eight news now
 

